From 6ea5d1c86a7e85264898befcd73991fb33ed4842 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nate MacFadden <natemacfadden@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:39:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] cpu-simple: experimental exact fast-forward of guest spin
 loops

A guest thread that polls memory for a GPU completion signal spends
most of a GPU kernel's simulated time in a tight loop that re-reads
unchanged memory. On an AtomicSimpleCPU every iteration is simulated
in full.

With the new, experimental BaseAtomicSimpleCPU parameter
spin_fast_forward (default False; a warning is printed when it is
set), the CPU detects a loop iteration that repeats exactly: the same
fetch/load/store stream with the same data, the same integer,
floating-point, condition-code and misc registers at the loop head,
no locked, uncacheable, LL/SC or AMO access, no serializing or
non-speculative instruction (rdtsc, cpuid, m5 ops), no fault or
stall, no pending interrupt and an empty instruction-count event
queue. After two such iterations it measures what one iteration adds
to every stat, stops its tick event at the loop head and waits.

Anything that could change what the loop observes ends the skip
first: a write by another agent to any cache line the loop fetches,
reads or writes (AbstractMemory::access()/functionalAccess() and DMA
backdoor writes), an x86 interrupt request, a stats dump or reset, a
drain, a read of the instruction or op count (scheduleInstStop(),
totalInsts(), progress events), a simulation-loop exit and a Python
event. At wake tick T it credits k whole iterations (k times the
measured deltas of every CPU, TLB, crossbar and DRAM stat, plus the
instruction and op counters), replays the leftover slots, at least
one whole iteration, at their own ticks and reschedules the tick
event at the next slot tick.

No skip starts where skipped iterations would leave state behind that
cannot be credited: with a classic cache, a SimpleMemory with
latency_var, a probe listener on the CPU (SimPoint, LoopPoint, PMU),
a progress interval, a branch predictor, an enabled debug flag, or
another CPU switched in. A loop whose iteration changes a stat that
cannot be credited (for example a coherent crossbar's snoop fanout
Distribution) is remembered and not measured again. A stats reset
during a skip or a measurement ends the skip or restarts the
measurement, so no pre-reset work lands in the new section.

The detector's state is process-global, so the parameter may be set on
only one CPU, which must be a single-threaded X86 CPU; anything else is
fatal(). It is validated only for single-CPU X86 AtomicSimpleCPU runs,
and not tested on no-ISA (NULL) builds. Known difference: checkpoints
taken after a skip hold smaller x86 TLB LRU sequence numbers (same
relative order, so a restored run behaves the same).

Invariant: credited deltas equal what the skipped iterations would
have produced, and every event that can change the loop's inputs, or
read its outputs, ends the skip before it takes effect, so all stats
and the simulated timeline are identical to running the loop.

Measured (gem5.opt, enabled vs. not): GEMM n=512 GEMM-section host
time -6.7% and GEMV n=2048 -11.2% (2 paired runs each); GEMM n=128
-33% (one run each).
The refusals and extra wake sources cost under 1% on these runs
(GEMM n=512 -0.1%, GEMV n=2048 -0.9%, 2 interleaved pairs each).

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
